            <ArticleTitle>Acute myopia induced by topiramate: report of a case and review of the literature.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>108-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Topiramate, a new anticonvulsant, is also used for the prophylaxis of migraine and cluster headache. A serious but not often discussed side effect of the drug is the development of acute myopia and acute angle-closure glaucoma in the early stage of therapy that subsides rapidly with prompt discontinuation. One such case is reported here and the relevant literature in this regard is also reviewed.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
